# THE TOOLING WRITES LF AND THEN COMPARES BYTE FOR BYTE, so the working copy has
# to hold what the writers emit. On Windows, core.autocrlf=true smudged the
# generated shards to CRLF on checkout and `build-data.mjs --check` - which reads
# them as text and compares them to a fresh build - failed on a tree nobody had
# edited. A merge was enough to trigger it, which makes the check read as "your
# data is stale" when the only difference is a line ending git itself inserted.
#
# eol=lf pins the working copy for everything git detects as text. Binary is
# auto-detected and untouched; the archive's PDFs are named anyway, because a
# PDF that ever gets line-ending translation is a corrupted PDF.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.pdf binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
